WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.240
Throughout human evolution,
eyes have been vital to our survival.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.400
They've evolved to be round

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:07.640
so that they can swivel
to spot delicious prey

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:09.480
and easily roll when your cousin

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.400
talks about his new car
for the third time.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.960
But to get to that point,
our peepers have come a long way.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.880
They most likely developed from
a tiny light-sensitive patch

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.200
on single-celled organisms that
would help them move towards light

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:23.760
in the hope of finding food.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.920
As the creatures evolved,
this patch started to curve inward

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.720
to better focus light. And now, a
whole bunch of evolution later,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.000
what we've arrived at
are two twinkling spheres

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.920
that are capable of seeing
about a million colours.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:39.600
Not bad, eh?

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:41.640
We might actually be on
to something with that

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:43.720
'window to the soul' stuff too,

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.240
as our eyes can portray more about
our emotions than you might think.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.120
Your pupils dilate when you're
scared, nervous or excited.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.280
That could be why they enlarge
when we make eye contact

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.640
with someone we, like,
like-like.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.640
Research has also shown that
even making eye contact with

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.400
a portrait painting sets off
a whole raft of brain activity.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:08.880
Our eyes are also responsible for
another universal emotional reaction

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.880
- a good old weep.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.120
But we actually have
three types of tears,

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.360
each with their own
chemical makeup.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.760
Basal, which lubricate the eye.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.200
Reflex tears, caused by irritants
like dust or chopped onions.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.320
And psychic, or emotional tears,

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.560
the kind that appear when
you find out about Bambi's mum

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.080
for the first time.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.120
When it comes to our pupils, it's not
just the size of them that varies -

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.920
the shape of an animal's pupils
can tell us if it's predator or prey.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.520
Some have horizonal pupils,
making them more adept at scanning

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.240
for predators while
munching on grass...

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.120
found on your local
goats, sheep, and moose.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.000
Vertical pupils like those found
on foxes, cats and crocodiles

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:54.480
are better for ambushing and hunting
at night, and have become synonymous

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.320
with animals that you just
do not want to mess with.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:01.440
Our round pupils are
kind of a best of both worlds

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.600
and help us in active foraging
and chasing down prey.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.680
Which, you know,
we totally do, all the time.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.520
There are quite a few animals who do
a better job of seeing than we do.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.400
The elephant hawk-moth,

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.200
which sounds like that band
your dad might have been in,

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.120
was the first animal proven
to be able to see colour

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.440
in the dead of night.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.880
Their large, sensitive eyes
allow the maximum

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:24.480
available light to pass through.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:28.840
However, this does impact their
ability to detect fast-moving objects

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.520
like a rolled-up newspaper.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.560
Human eyes might not be perfect.
Parts of them are even useless,

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.320
like that little pink bit
in the inner corners.
\N

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.400
That's the remains of a third eyelid

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.840
like the ones cats,
dogs and camels have.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.080
Our eyes aren't just physically
interesting, but culturally too.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:50.840
Much loved by conspiracy theorists
the world over, The Eye of Horus

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.640
may symbolise the Illuminati to some,
but its origins lie in Egypt

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:58.520
where the eye is a symbol
representing sacrifice,

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:00.160
healing and protection.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:03.640
In Turkey, some believe that
a sideways glance can cause

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.400
genuine pain.
And thinking back to high school,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.320
they're not wrong.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.280
Glass pendants, also eye-shaped,
are worn to protect

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:13.240
against this evil eye.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:17.880
So whether round, monolid, hooded,
downturned, upturned or almond,

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.640
words you're probably familiar with
if you spend as much time watching

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:24.800
makeup tutorials as me,
your eyes are unique to you.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.760
The two are even unique
to each other.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.840
But one thing does unite us all...

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:32.280
we can't for the life of us
keep them open when we sneeze.

